1. Who Is NexaBank (hypothetical name)?
NexaBank is a mid-sized national retail and commercial bank founded in Charlotte, North Carolina
in 1987. What started as a community savings institution has grown, through decades of careful
expansion and a handful of strategic acquisitions, into one of the top 30 banks in the United States.
Today it serves 4.2 million customers across 38 states through 520 branches and a digital platform
that now handles the majority of daily customer interactions.
Cards are the heartbeat of NexaBank's business. Every month, 35 million card transactions flow
through its infrastructure — debit, credit, and commercial cards operating under Visa and
Mastercard. Card interchange income alone accounts for 22% of the bank's total non-interest
revenue, making it the single most important fee-generating product the bank operates.
NexaBank is not a technology laggard. It has a functioning mainframe core, a capable card
processing setup, and a seasoned fraud operations team of 250 people. But it was built for a
different era. Its fraud detection engine — FIS Falcon, deployed in 2014 — was designed around
static rules and batch processing. In 2014, that was enough. In 2025, it is not.
2. The Problem
In January 2025, NexaBank's Chief Risk Officer, John Doe, was reviewing the bank's year-end
fraud report when a number stopped her cold: $38.4 million. That was the total direct fraud loss for
2024 — up 34% from the year before, and more than double what it had been three years earlier.
The trend line was going in one direction only.
Margaret pulled her team together for a three-day deep dive. What they found was not one problem
but four, each feeding the others.
Problem 1 — The Fraud Engine Is Losing
NexaBank's fraud rate of 0.32% is more than four times the rate achieved by AI-powered banks
operating at best practice (~0.08%). Card-not-present fraud, synthetic identity fraud, and account
takeover attacks have all accelerated. The Falcon rule engine catches patterns it was explicitly
programmed to detect — but modern fraudsters have learned to stay just below every threshold it
monitors. The bank is fighting an adaptive enemy with a static weapon.
Problem 2 — The Bank Is Blocking Its Own Customers
Falcon flags 12% of all transactions as suspicious. Of those, 94% are completely legitimate. That
means NexaBank is incorrectly declining nearly 4 million valid transactions every month — costing
$7.2M in lost interchange revenue and, worse, destroying customer trust. Twenty-two percent of
customers who experience a false decline close their account within 90 days. The fraud system has
become a customer attrition engine.
Problem 3 — Fraud Rings Are Invisible
NexaBank's fraud team reviews 48,000 alerts every day, manually, with tools that show individual
transactions but not the connections between them. In Q3 2024, a coordinated ring of 847 synthetic
accounts stole $4.2M over eight months before being discovered — and only because a ring
member reported it. Every single account in that ring looked clean on its own. Only the pattern of
shared devices, shared merchants, and shared money flows revealed the ring. NexaBank had no
tool to see that pattern.
The board was presented with the full picture in February 2025. Total annual fraud exposure —
direct losses, false decline revenue foregone, investigation costs, and customer attrition — came to
$161 million. The board approved an immediate strategic investment to build a next-generation AI
fraud platform. NexaBank's transformation begins here.
3. What NexaBank Is Thinking of Building
NexaBank's technology leadership has identified a clear direction: replace the static, rule-based
Falcon engine with an AI-powered platform that learns continuously, scores transactions in real
time, and surfaces hidden fraud ring patterns that no rule engine could ever detect. Rather than
buying a vendor solution, the bank wants to build and own its core AI capability — treating its fraud
data and its trained models as proprietary competitive assets.
At the centre of the proposed platform is a custom-trained Large Language Model (LLM) and an
ensemble of ML models, working together across the full transaction lifecycle.
The Custom LLM — What It Will Do
The LLM is not a chatbot. It is an intelligent reasoning layer that sits on top of the numerical fraud
scores produced by the ML models and performs three jobs that no scoring model alone can do:
•
•
Interpret the score in context — the ML model returns a number. The LLM turns that number,
combined with the customer's history, the merchant context, and the risk pattern, into a
plain-English explanation that an investigator can read and act on in seconds.
Handle ambiguous middle-ground cases — a score of 62 or 71 is genuinely uncertain. The LLM is
trained to reason through the specific combination of signals and make a defensible decision
recommendation, not just apply a threshold.
•
Compose real-time customer communications — when a transaction is declined or challenged, the
LLM drafts the notification to the customer, tailored to the specific reason, in a tone appropriate to
their customer tier and history.
